+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql cannot be applied to the prod snapshot
+
+## What was asked
+
+`users.email` must become `NOT NULL` and unique case-insensitively. The
+migration must not modify or delete any customer rows
+(`test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` asserts the rows are identical before and
+after, and Support owns the data).
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` already violates both requirements:
+
+- **Required (NOT NULL):** id 17 (`Goran Okafor`) has `email = NULL`.
+- **Unique (case-insensitive):** ids 1, 4 and 23 all share the same address
+  ignoring case:
+  - 1: `ana.silva@example.com`
+  - 4: `Ana.Silva@example.com`
+  - 23: `ana.silva@example.com`
+
+Verified with:
+
+```sql
+SELECT id, name FROM users WHERE email IS NULL;
+-- (17, 'Goran Okafor')
+
+SELECT lower(email), group_concat(id)
+FROM users WHERE email IS NOT NULL
+GROUP BY lower(email) HAVING count(*) > 1;
+-- ('ana.silva@example.com', '1,4,23')
+```
+
+SQLite validates existing rows when a `NOT NULL` constraint or unique index
+is created. On this snapshot that validation necessarily fails. The only ways
+to make the migration succeed are to:
+
+1. set a value for id 17 (modifies a customer row), or
+2. change/clear one or more of ids 1, 4, 23 (modifies customer rows), or
+3. delete duplicate rows (deletes customer rows).
+
+All three are explicitly forbidden. There is no schema-level way to declare
+the column required and unique while leaving these rows untouched. Triggers
+or partial indexes would only enforce the rules for future writes and would
+leave the existing data invalid, so they would be a workaround rather than
+the requested constraint; I have not used one.
+
+## What must happen before this migration can be written
+
+Support needs to clean the production data:
+
+- give id 17 a valid email (or otherwise resolve the missing address);
+- decide which of ids 1, 4 and 23 is the canonical record and reconcile the
+  other two (for example by correcting their addresses), without losing
+  customer rows.
+
+Once the snapshot contains no `NULL` emails and no case-insensitive
+duplicates, `002_unique_email.sql` can be implemented (for example by
+rebuilding the table with `email TEXT NOT NULL` plus
+`CREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_unique ON users(lower(email))`).
+
+## Current file state
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` deliberately contains only an explanatory
+comment and no DDL, so it cannot silently claim to have enforced a constraint
+that the data does not satisfy.
+
+`test_constraint_on_fresh_db` fails as expected (the constraint is not
+implemented); `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` passes because no rows are
+touched.
